    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Nahuatl, a Uto-Aztecan language that came to central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> from the north, was the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> and the common language of trade and empire across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>.</li><li>After the Spanish conquest it was written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet, producing a large body of Indigenous writing, from chronicles, poetry and wills to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a>, one of the greatest records of a society before European rule.</li><li>Still spoken by about 1.7 million people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, it has given words such as chocolate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomato">tomato</a>, avocado and chili to languages around the world.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> takes its name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a>, the Nahuatl-speaking founders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, and the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> comes from a Nahuatl word meaning “place of many trees”.</p></aside>
    </section>

