    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 900–1150</span><span class="rf-text">Tula, the city of the Toltecs, whose people probably spoke Nahuatl, dominates central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1325</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, by tradition, on an island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1519</span><span class="rf-text">Malintzin, a Nahuatl-speaking woman, becomes the interpreter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1536</span><span class="rf-text">The College of Santa Cruz de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a> opens, teaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Spanish and Nahuatl to young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> nobles</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1571</span><span class="rf-text">Alonso de Molina publishes his great dictionary of Nahuatl and Spanish</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1577</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardino-de-sahagun">Bernardino de Sahagún</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> scholars complete the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1770</span><span class="rf-text">A royal decree of Charles III calls for Spanish to replace the Indigenous languages of the empire</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2003</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>’s law on linguistic rights recognises Nahuatl and other Indigenous languages as national languages</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan.jpg" alt="Tenochtitlan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_completa_de_Tenochtitl%C3%A1n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">thomas kole, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nahuatl belongs to the Uto-Aztecan family, which stretches from the Great Basin of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshone">Shoshone</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> were spoken, through the Hopi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqui">Yaqui</a> and Huichol of northern and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, to its southernmost branch, the Nahuan languages of central Mexico and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. Linguists believe that speakers of early Nahuan moved south from northern Mexico into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> in several waves, perhaps from around 500 CE, borrowing words from the older languages of the region as they settled among their peoples.</p><p>Nahuatl speakers rose to power in central Mexico in the following centuries. The Toltecs of Tula, who dominated the region from about 900 to 1150 and whom later peoples regarded as the founders of civilisation, probably spoke Nahuatl. After the fall of Tula, groups of Nahuatl speakers, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca">Chichimecs</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, migrated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a>, who according to their tradition came from a place called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztlan">Aztlan</a> and founded their city, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, on an island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a> in 1325.</p><p>In 1428 Tenochtitlan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a> formed the Triple Alliance, which in less than a century built the largest empire of Mesoamerica. Nahuatl became the language of its government, tribute and trade, and of the travelling merchants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pochteca">pochteca</a>, who carried it far beyond its homeland, as far as Central America, where related Nahuan languages were already spoken by peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pipil">Pipil</a>.</p><p>The form of the language spoken in Tenochtitlan and Texcoco, known today as Classical Nahuatl, was admired for its elegance, and Texcoco, under its poet-king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nezahualcoyotl">Nezahualcoyotl</a>, who lived from 1402 to 1472, was famed as a centre of refined speech and learning. The name Nahuatl is related to a word meaning “clear” or “audible”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="structure">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/codex-mendoza"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/codex-mendoza.jpg" alt="Codex Mendoza" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/codex-mendoza">Codex Mendoza</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CodexMendoza01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nahuatl has a distinctive sound system. Its best-known sound is tl, pronounced as a single consonant with the tongue against the roof of the mouth and the air escaping at its sides, which appears in the name of the language itself; it also has a glottal stop and long and short vowels, but no sounds b, d or g.</p><p>Its grammar is agglutinative and, in part, polysynthetic: words are built from roots and many prefixes and suffixes, and a whole sentence can be expressed in a single word. A verb can absorb its object: ninacacua means “I eat meat”, from ni-, “I”, naca-, “meat”, and cua, “eat”. Nouns often end in a suffix, -tl, -tli or -li, as in tomatl, “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomato">tomato</a>”, and chīlli, “chili”. Nahuatl has an elaborate system of reverential forms: the suffix -tzin, added to names and words, expresses respect or affection, and whole verbs change to speak of or to people of high rank, as in the formal speeches of the elders, the huehuetlatolli.</p><p>Before the conquest, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahuas</a> recorded their history, tribute, calendars and rituals in painted books, the codices, in a pictorial system that combined images with glyphs for names, places and dates. Many glyphs were read by their sounds, as in a rebus: the glyph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> shows a prickly-pear cactus, nochtli, growing from a stone, tetl. Scholars debate how far this system could record spoken sentences, but it was used together with oral recitation to preserve knowledge.</p><p>After the conquest, Spanish friars and Nahua scholars adapted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet to Nahuatl, using letters and combinations such as x for the sound sh, tz, tl and hu. In 1645 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> Horacio Carochi published a grammar that marked vowel length and the glottal stop, the most precise description of the classical language.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="speakers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Speakers &amp; Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nahuas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nahuas.jpg" alt="Nahuas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahuas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ni%C3%B1as_Nahuas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fernando Rosales, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the time of the Spanish conquest, Nahuatl was the most widely spoken language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> and its main common language. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> landed in 1519, he was given Malintzin, a woman who spoke both Nahuatl and Maya, who became his interpreter and adviser; Nahuatl-speaking allies, above all the people of Tlaxcala, fought beside the Spanish in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> in 1521.</p><p>Under Spanish rule, Nahuatl remained the language of most people in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> for centuries. The friars learned it to preach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and used it as a common language for evangelisation even among non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> peoples, and in 1570 King Philip II made it the general language for instructing the Indigenous peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. The College of Santa Cruz de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a>, founded in 1536, taught young Nahua nobles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Spanish, and its trilingual graduates worked with the friars as scholars, translators and writers.</p><p>Nahua towns kept their records in their own language for nearly three centuries: municipal council minutes, land sales, wills, petitions to the authorities and annals of local history, thousands of which survive in archives. In 1770, however, a royal decree of Charles III called for Spanish to replace the Indigenous languages, and written Nahuatl declined in the late colonial period.</p><p>After Mexican independence in 1821, Spanish was the language of the nation, and Nahuatl speakers became a minority, often facing discrimination and pressure to abandon their language. Today about 1.7 million people speak Nahuatl, the largest number for any Indigenous language of Mexico, in the states of Puebla, Veracruz, Hidalgo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> and others and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>; in 2003 a law recognised it and the other Indigenous languages as national languages of Mexico.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="literature">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Literature &amp; Learning</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florentine-codex.jpg" alt="Florentine Codex" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_florentino_51_9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernardino de Sahagún, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nahuatl has one of the largest bodies of written literature of any Indigenous language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Before the conquest, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahuas</a> cultivated an oral literature of songs, speeches and histories; poetry was called in xochitl in cuicatl, “flower and song”, and the elders’ speeches, the huehuetlatolli, taught moral conduct in a highly formal style.</p><p>After the conquest, much of this tradition was written down. The Cantares Mexicanos, a 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of songs, preserves poems attributed to rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nezahualcoyotl">Nezahualcoyotl</a>, meditating on the brevity of life and the mystery of the divine. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a>, completed around 1577 by the Franciscan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardino-de-sahagun">Bernardino de Sahagún</a> with Nahua scholars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a>, is an encyclopaedia of Aztec civilisation in Nahuatl and Spanish, whose twelfth book tells the story of the conquest from the perspective of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a>.</p><p>Nahua historians wrote annals of their cities and peoples. The most prolific, Domingo Francisco de San Antón Muñón <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimalpahin">Chimalpahin</a>, in the early 17th century, wrote histories of Chalco and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> from Indigenous and Spanish sources, and the Annals of Tlatelolco and the Codex Chimalpopoca preserve histories and myths. Religious literature includes sermons, plays and the Nican mopohua, the Nahuatl account of the apparitions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-guadalupe">Virgin of Guadalupe</a>, published in 1649.</p><p>Alonso de Molina’s dictionary of 1571 and Carochi’s grammar of 1645 are monuments of linguistic scholarship. In the 20th and 21st centuries, scholars such as Miguel León-Portilla made Nahuatl literature known to a wide public, and Nahua writers have created a new literature in their language.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="change">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Change &amp; Descendants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/popocatepetl"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/popocatepetl.jpg" alt="Popocatépetl" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/popocatepetl">Popocatépetl</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mexico-Popocatepetl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jakub Hejtmánek, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Nahuatl spoken today is not a single language but a group of varieties, some of them hard for speakers of others to understand, spoken in different regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>; they descend from the dialects of the 16th century rather than from the Classical Nahuatl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> alone. Over five centuries of contact, Nahuatl has borrowed many Spanish words, and its speakers are now almost all bilingual in Spanish.</p><p>Spanish, in turn, absorbed hundreds of Nahuatl words, especially in Mexico: aguacate, “avocado”, tomate, chile, chocolate, cacahuate, “peanut”, tamal, elote, “corn on the cob”, guajolote, “turkey”, papalote, “kite”, and tianguis, “market”. Countless place names in Mexico and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> are Nahuatl: Mexico itself, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a> and the volcano <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popocatepetl">Popocatépetl</a>, “smoking mountain”.</p><p>Through Spanish, Nahuatl words entered English and many other languages: chocolate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomato">tomato</a>, avocado, chili, guacamole, coyote, ocelot and axolotl. They reflect the plants, foods and animals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> that spread across the world after 1492.</p><p>Like many Indigenous languages, Nahuatl is losing speakers as young people shift to Spanish, although its absolute number of speakers has remained large. Teaching in schools, university programmes, radio, literature and online media are part of efforts to strengthen it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nahuatl lies at the heart of Mexican identity. The country’s name, its capital’s history, the eagle on the cactus at the centre of its flag, the names of its places, foods and gods, and much of its everyday vocabulary come from the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>.</p><p>The colonial Nahuatl documents are among the richest sources for the history of any Indigenous people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. They allow historians to see Mesoamerican society and the colonial world through the eyes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahuas</a> themselves, in their own words, and have transformed the study of colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><p>The poetry of “flower and song” and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a> are recognised as treasures of world literature and ethnography, and the Nahuatl words for the foods of the Americas, from chocolate and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomato">tomato</a> to avocado and chili, are spoken around the world every day.</p><p>With some 1.7 million speakers it remains one of the most widely spoken Indigenous languages of the Americas, and its speakers and writers continue to make it a living language of culture.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Nahuatl, the Uto-Aztecan language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, was the common language of trade and empire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> and, written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet after the Spanish conquest, produced one of the richest Indigenous literatures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, from annals and poetry to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a>. Spoken by about 1.7 million people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> today, it has given the world words such as chocolate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomato">tomato</a> and avocado.</em></p>

